{"decisions":[{"i":0,"decision":"F0","origin":null,"reason":"Accurately identifies both estimates and their respective methods.","sourceEvidence":"The seismic analysis suggested 7.7; the geodetic approach gave 7.9 using ground deformation.","synthesisStatement":"The seismic analysis suggested 7.7; the geodetic approach used ground deformation and gave an estimate of 7.9.","narrativeStatement":"The 1906 earthquake has estimates of magnitude 7.7 and 7.9 from two different approaches: one based on seismic recordings and another on geodetic measurements of ground deformation.","materiality":"None."},{"i":1,"decision":"F0","origin":null,"reason":"The stated explanation and disagreement between methods are supported.","sourceEvidence":"Different methods of estimating earthquake size can disagree.","synthesisStatement":"Different methods of estimating earthquake size can disagree.","narrativeStatement":"The story explains how the methods differ and why estimates of earthquake size can disagree.","materiality":"None."},{"i":2,"decision":"F0","origin":null,"reason":"Faithfully conveys that usable data came from 12 of the 96 observatories studied.","sourceEvidence":"Recordings were studied from 96 observatories; usable data from 12 were digitized and modeled.","synthesisStatement":"One team studied seismic recordings from 96 observatories and digitized and modeled usable data from 12.","narrativeStatement":"The seismic analysis, which suggested 7.7 using usable recordings from 12 of 96 observatories studied.","materiality":"None."},{"i":3,"decision":"F0","origin":null,"reason":"Faithfully attributes the 7.9 estimate to the USGS geodetic re-evaluation and its benchmark angle and distance measurements.","sourceEvidence":"USGS re-evaluated geodetic measurements using benchmark distances and angles; the estimate was 7.9.","synthesisStatement":"The geodetic approach used ground deformation reflected in angles and distances between benchmarks and gave an estimate of 7.9.","narrativeStatement":"The USGS re-evaluation of geodetic measurements, which estimated 7.9 from changes in angles and distances between benchmarks.","materiality":"None."}]}

